error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central mechanism. ETFs can meet redemptions by delivering appreciated stock to authorized participants instead of selling it. It says qualifying in-kind redemptions generally do not trigger fund-level gain recognition under RIC rules, which is the substance of 852(b)(6); a citation is not required. It notes that low-basis lots can be delivered out to reduce embedded gains, and that cash-dealing mutual funds more often must sell holdings, realizing gains that are distributed. The limits are well covered: no guarantee of zero capital-gain distributions; dividends and gains from portfolio changes remain taxable; investors still owe tax when selling ETF shares at a gain; and the benefit depends on flows, turnover and in-kind redemption capacity, which approximates 'deliverable assets and the transaction.' One minor gap is that it never says outright that the treatment is not an ETF-only legal exemption, for example that mutual funds can also redeem in kind. However, framing it as a RIC rule and noting that mutual funds can sometimes avoid sales largely implies this, so it is not a substantive omission. No material errors.\"}","ttft_ms":3785,"type":"result","duration_ms":7029,"uuid":"79a3ad9c-0dab-4938-8dd6-b4d28a6f3959","ttft_stream_ms":813,"time_to_request_ms":65,"first_content_frame_ms":813,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
